Q: What is wrong with using `forward` without the `std::` qualifier? A: <p>The reasons for <code>std::forward</code> are the same as for <code>std::move</code>. Its a common name and it accepts any value. An unqualified call opens the door to ADL.</p>
<pre><code>namespace foo {
    struct foo {};
    template &lt;typename T&gt; void forward(foo&amp;) {}
};

int main() {
    foo::foo f;
    using std::forward;
    auto g = forward&lt;decltype(f)&gt;(f);   // calls foo::forward
    auto h = std::forward&lt;decltype(f)&gt;(f);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Neither <code>std::move</code> nor <code>std::forward</code> are sensible customization points. They both are just shorthand for a cast that would be confusing to replace with something else.</p>
<p>One shouldnt write something like <code>foo::forward</code>, but suppose its from a library predating C++11. Or its a recent library and nobody ever noticed it could be an issue (eg. because they are following the recomendation for fully qualified calls to <code>std::forward</code>).</p>
<p>Note that the example is chosen such that a compiler error is produced. If <code>foo::forward</code> had the same signature one can easily overlook that <code>auto g = forward&lt;decltype(f)&gt;(f)</code> does not call <code>std::forward</code>.</p>
<p>Don't think of <code>using std::forward</code> merely as a way of typing less. Its not just that. It enables ADL. Sometimes thats useful. Not here.</p>

Q: How should I handle memory management around a struct&#39;s heap-allocated member? A: <p><code>my_struct.description = ...</code> cannot be the ownership operation if <code>description</code> is only a <code>[]const u8</code>. A slice is only a pointer plus a length, so it has no place to record whether <code>deinit</code> should call <code>allocator.free</code> for that memory.</p>
<p>Pick one invariant. For the API described here, keep a setter for the owning case and make borrowed values explicit:</p>
<pre><code>zig
const MyStruct = struct {
    description: []const u8,
    owned_description: ?[]u8,

    pub fn init(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, input_str: []const u8) !MyStruct {
        const copy = try allocator.dupe(u8, input_str);
        return .{
            .description = copy,
            .owned_description = copy,
        };
    }

    pub fn initBorrowed(description: []const u8) MyStruct {
        return .{
            .description = description,
            .owned_description = null,
        };
    }

    pub fn deinit(self: *MyStruct, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) void {
        self.freeOwnedDescription(allocator);
        self.* = undefined;
    }

    fn freeOwnedDescription(self: *MyStruct, allocator: std.mem.Allocator) void {
        if (self.owned_description) |owned| {
            allocator.free(owned);
            self.owned_description = null;
        }
    }

    pub fn setDescription(self: *MyStruct, allocator: std.mem.Allocator, description: []const u8) !void {
        const copy = try allocator.dupe(u8, description);
        self.freeOwnedDescription(allocator);
        self.description = copy;
        self.owned_description = copy;
    }

    pub fn setDescriptionBorrowed(self: *MyStruct, allocator: std.mem.Allocator, description: []const u8) void {
        self.freeOwnedDescription(allocator);
        self.description = description;
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Usage is still one line at the call site:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>try my_struct.setDescription(allocator, &quot;some other text&quot;); // copies; MyStruct owns it
my_struct.setDescriptionBorrowed(allocator, shared_description); // caller owns/lifetime-manages it
</code></pre>
<p>Direct assignment to <code>description</code> is not ownership transfer. If callers need to replace the value, route that through a method that first releases the old owned buffer and then records whether the new value is owned or borrowed.</p>
